{%- comment -%}
  Everything under the buy box, as ONE PAGE.

  THIS REPLACES `product-tabs.liquid`, deleted 2026-09-13 on instruction: the
  four tabs — Details, Size & fit, Fabric & care, Shipping — were shown in a
  screenshot and the answer was "itu ga usah deh, dibikin 1 page aja". So the
  panels are sections of a page now, each under its own heading, all of them
  readable without operating anything.

  It is worth knowing what was given up, because the tabs were not lazy work:
  they were real ARIA tabs with the full keyboard contract — arrows between
  tabs, Home and End to the ends, one tab stop for the strip. None of that is
  needed now, which is the point. The behaviour went out of assets/theme.js with
  them rather than being left in unused, and `[data-tabs]` went out of the
  `scripting: none` block in theme-src/theme.css for the same reason.

  What it buys, beyond the instruction: the no-script rendering and the normal
  rendering are now the same page. The tabs had two — every panel in sequence
  with the strip removed, for a reader without script — and a page with one
  shape cannot drift between them.

  ORDER IS UNCHANGED, and it was argued for once already. Size & fit sits
  second, ahead of care: the brief lists the size chart before care
  instructions, and PRODUCT.md records fit as the purchase decision itself —
  almost every one of the twenty customer reviews names a height and a weight
  before it names a size. Moving it down is a change to what the page
  prioritises, not a tidy-up.

  THE SIZE CHART HAS TWO SOURCES AND THE PER-PRODUCT ONE WINS. `custom.size_chart`
  is a rich-text metafield a product can carry for a cut that does not follow the
  house measurements; with none, `size-guide.liquid` renders the shared chart
  from theme settings. That precedence is the same shape as the Shopee link's —
  the specific beats the general, and the general is a courtesy rather than the
  intended state.

  THERE ARE NOW TWO SHARED CHARTS, trousers and tops, and this snippet picks
  between them by `product.type` — see the liquid block below. Before
  2026-09-27 there was one, so the four tops were being shown trouser
  measurements against sizes they do not sell.

  Every section renders its own labelled placeholder when its copy is blank, so
  the page keeps its height and its shape before the studio has written a word.
  `description` is still blank across this catalogue on purpose: the design
  reused one generic paragraph for all eleven pieces, which would state a wrong
  inseam on most of them.

  FABRIC & CARE NO LONGER FALLS TO THAT PLACEHOLDER, as of 2026-09-27. The same
  objection applied to it — one paragraph cannot describe eight fabrics — and it
  was answered by keying the copy to the product's own `custom.material` rather
  than by writing one. See `fabric-care.liquid`. Details still has no such
  escape, because a description is per-product by nature.

  Accepts: product, shipping (store-wide policy copy)
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  # A PRODUCT'S OWN CHART, AND WHY THE TOPS NEED ONE.
  #
  # `custom.size_chart` holds the whole table: the first line is the headings,
  # every line after it is a row, all of them pipe separated. It is parsed here
  # rather than in size-guide because the heading row has to come off the top
  # before the rest can be handed over as an array, and Liquid cannot rejoin
  # lines with a newline once they are split.
  #
  # The four tops each carry one. They are one size apiece with their own
  # figures, and Rui is a two-piece set whose sweater and cardigan measure
  # differently, so a single store-wide tops table put four products' rows on
  # every one of their pages. That is what this replaces.
  #
  # The trousers still share a chart, because they share a size system: seven
  # sizes cut to the same house measurements across thirteen products.
  assign chart_raw = product.metafields.custom.size_chart.value
  assign chart_note = product.metafields.custom.size_note.value
  assign own_columns = ''
  assign own_rows = ''
  if chart_raw != blank
    assign chart_lines = chart_raw | strip | newline_to_br
    assign chart_lines = chart_lines | replace: '<br />', '|~|' | replace: '<br>', '|~|'
    assign chart_lines = chart_lines | split: '|~|'
    assign own_columns = chart_lines.first | replace: '|', ','
    assign own_rows = chart_lines | slice: 1, 30
  endif

  # The literal 'Tops' is a coupling to a product_type value in the Shopify
  # admin and it is the only one in the theme. It exists so a top with no chart
  # of its own draws a placeholder rather than the TROUSER chart, which would
  # offer Waist / Hip / Thigh against sizes the product does not sell. Rename
  # that type and a chartless top silently gets the trouser table back, so if
  # the taxonomy ever changes, this line changes with it.
  assign is_tops = false
  if product.type == 'Tops'
    assign is_tops = true
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  DETAILS IS GONE, deleted on instruction, and the page now opens on Size & fit.
  It rendered `product.description`, which since the Shopee import is the
  studio's full listing copy: fabric, size run and care all in one block. That
  made it a long preamble in front of the section a shopper actually scrolls
  for, and it repeated what Fabric & care says underneath. The description is
  still on the product in Shopify and still the source of everything below.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="flex flex-col divide-y divide-rule border-t border-rule">
  <section class="flex flex-col gap-7 py-8" aria-labelledby="detail-fit">
    <h2 id="detail-fit" class="text-micro uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-subtle">
      {{ 'product.size_and_fit' | t }}
    </h2>

    {%- comment -%}
      The chart is deliberately NOT inside `wl-measure`. A measure caps the line
      length at something readable for prose, and a table of seven sizes against
      five measurements is not prose — capping it would force the columns
      narrower than the numbers and start the sideways scroll early.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h3 class="text-caption uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-muted">{{ 'product.size_chart' | t }}</h3>
      {%- if own_columns != blank -%}
        {% render 'size-guide', columns: own_columns, rows_array: own_rows, note: chart_note %}
      {%- elsif is_tops -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          A top with no chart of its own gets the placeholder, never the
          trouser chart. A wrong table is worse than a missing one: it reads as
          a measurement the shopper can act on.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {% render 'copy', value: '', label: 'size chart', lines: 2 %}
      {%- else -%}
        {% render 'size-guide' %}
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      Fit information is gone, removed on instruction along with the material
      line. `custom.fit` was never defined on this store, so this block drew a
      heading over two grey placeholder bars on every product page, and the
      measurements a shopper actually needs are in the size chart directly
      above it and in the description. The metafield is still read by nothing
      now; if it is ever defined, this block is what to put back.
    {%- endcomment -%}
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4 py-8" aria-labelledby="detail-care">
    <h2 id="detail-care" class="text-micro uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-subtle">
      {{ 'product.fabric_and_care' | t }}
    </h2>
    {%- comment -%}
      THIS WAS A BARE PLACEHOLDER ON ALL SEVENTEEN PRODUCTS UNTIL 2026-09-27.
      `custom.care` is undefined on this store, so every product page rendered
      three grey bars under a heading that promised a fabric explanation. The
      section comment above still describes why the metafield is blank on
      purpose — one generic paragraph across the whole catalogue would state a
      wrong fabric on most of it — and that reasoning is intact: what changed is
      that the copy is now keyed BY FABRIC, so there is no one generic
      paragraph. `fabric-care.liquid` carries the precedence and the argument.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="wl-measure text-body leading-body text-ink-body">
      {% render 'fabric-care', product: product %}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4 py-8" aria-labelledby="detail-shipping">
    <h2 id="detail-shipping" class="text-micro uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-subtle">
      {{ 'product.shipping' | t }}
    </h2>
    <div class="wl-measure text-body leading-body text-ink-body">
      {% render 'copy', value: shipping, label: 'shipping', lines: 3 %}
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
